  "metricCtlExplainer": "CTL is a 42-day weighted average of your training load: how much training you have absorbed, not a measure of fitness or health. It can keep rising while you dig yourself into a hole.",
  "metricAtlExplainer": "ATL is the same average over 7 days — the load still sitting in your legs. It rises and falls quickly, and a high ATL inside a hard block is normal rather than a warning on its own.",
  "metricFormExplainer": "Form is CTL minus ATL. Positive means you are carrying less recent load than usual, negative means recent training is heavy. Negative form is where fitness gets built; it is not a diagnosis.",
  "metricRampExplainer": "Ramp rate is how fast CTL is changing. There is no universally safe number — judge it against your own history, your running volume, and how you are sleeping and recovering.",
